\section{Use Traversal Order for Numbering, Not for Proof of Dominance}

In a graph with distinguished entry, \(a\) dominates \(b\) if
every entry-to-\(b\) path contains \(a\). In this chapter all
queries are on entry-reachable nodes. Unreachable nodes receive
no interval, even when an edge from one enters the reachable
region. Treating unreachable predecessors as already analyzed
can give arbitrary results. Entry can be its own immediate
dominator in the solver as a sentinel; it has no parent in the
published dominator tree. The tree stores the closest proper
dominator of every other reachable block. Muchnick presents
dominators by iterating sets as well as by a faster algorithm
\cite{muchnick1997advanced}. Here the iterative immediate-dominator
scheme of Cooper, Harvey, and Kennedy avoids storing one full
dominator set per block \cite{cooper2001simple}.

Number nodes in reverse DFS postorder from the entry on the
CFG. In \Cref{fig:visual-426}, DFS might temporarily discover
\(J\) from \(L\), but \(L\) cannot dominate \(J\) because
\(E,R,J\) is also a path. A CFG DFS timestamp answers a DFS
tree question, not a dominance question. Only after constructing
the dominator tree should we label its subtrees with intervals.

