% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Walk Immediate-Dominator Chains Toward Entry}

\begin{verbatim}
solve_idom(cfg, entry):
    order := reverse(dfs_postorder(cfg, entry))
    rank := index_by_block(order)        # entry is zero
    idom := all_missing()
    idom[entry] := entry
    repeat:
        changed := false
        for b in order except entry:
            known := [p for p in predecessors(b)
                        if idom[p] is not Missing]
            if known.empty(): continue
            parent := known.first
            for p in known.rest:
                parent := intersect(parent, p, idom, rank)
            if idom[b] != parent:
                idom[b] := parent
                changed := true
    until not changed
    return idom

intersect(a, b, idom, rank):
    while a != b:
        if rank[a] > rank[b]: a := idom[a]
        else: b := idom[b]
    return a
\end{verbatim}

DFS in this algorithm explores only reachable CFG nodes and
emits each on finish, not on discovery. Every nonentry node
has at least its DFS tree predecessor earlier in reverse
postorder, so it has one known predecessor by the time it is
first visited. Backedge predecessors may become usable later
and can require another sweep. Moving the larger rank upward
is correct for this numbering because the provisional parent
chains decrease toward the entry. If numbering instead grows
in postorder, the comparison must be reversed; ranks, DFS
times, and printed block numbers should have different names.
Use an explicit stack with successor cursors to obtain finish
order without overflowing the host call stack on a long chain.

\section{Answer Dominance With Half-Open Tree Intervals}

Gather child lists from the final immediate-dominator array,
omitting the root self-link. Walk that tree and assign an
increasing preorder number on entry. On leaving a node save
the next unused number as its subtree end. A block \(a\)
dominates \(b\) exactly when
\(\mathrm{in}[a]\leq\mathrm{in}[b]<\mathrm{end}[a]\).
Strict dominance excludes equality. The same numbering supports
enumeration of all blocks dominated by \(a\) as a contiguous
slice of the preorder array. These tree intervals are nested
or disjoint; an arbitrary interval index for overlapping spans
is not needed.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\hline
Block & Immediate dominator & Preorder slice & Dominated blocks \\
\hline
\(E\) & --- & \([0,4)\) & \(E,L,R,J\) \\
\(L\) & \(E\) & \([1,2)\) & \(L\) \\
\(R\) & \(E\) & \([2,3)\) & \(R\) \\
\(J\) & \(E\) & \([3,4)\) & \(J\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

For SSA uses, block dominance is only part of the legality
check. In the same block the defining instruction must be
before the ordinary operand use. An incoming value for a join,
on the other hand, is read at its predecessor's terminator.
The definition of a value passed on \(L\)'s edge need not
dominate \(J\). Do not compensate by weakening the dominance
query; give the use the right program point.

\section{Invalidate the Index on Graph Changes}

Remove \(E\to R\) from the diamond. If \(R\) becomes
unreachable, the only remaining path to \(J\) passes through
\(L\). The program still has a block handle for \(J\), and
perhaps for \(R\), but the old tree gives the wrong answer
about \(L\) and \(J\). Version the tree against control-flow
edits. Simple clients can rebuild it on demand; if using a
dynamic algorithm, update both the immediate dominators and
their labels or expose a different ancestry representation.
Saving the old ranks just because the block allocator did not
move any payload is not safe.

Postdominance needs its own graph and policy. Reverse traversal
from a chosen virtual exit handles multiple returns, but
blocks from which no exit is reachable do not participate in
the same tree. Exceptional edges, infinite loops, and synthetic
exits affect which paths the answer quantifies over. Declare
those choices before using postdominance for control dependence.

To test small graphs, independently compute full dominator
sets by initializing every nonentry reachable node to the
reachable universe and intersecting its reachable predecessors'
sets, adding the node itself. Compare every ordered pair to
the interval answer. Include the edited diamond, a loop with
a backedge, an unreachable predecessor pointing into a live
join, and disconnected components. Separately compare CFG
traversal output to the dominator-tree traversal on the diamond:
their differing results are an intentional consequence of
asking different graph questions.

\section{Publish an Index That Supports Both Membership and Walking}

\begin{verbatim}
index_tree(children, entry):
    preorder := []
    events := [Enter(entry)]
    while not events.empty():
        kind, b := events.pop()
        if kind == Enter:
            start[b] := preorder.length
            preorder.append(b)
            events.push(Leave(b))
            for c in reverse(children[b]):
                events.push(Enter(c))
        else:
            end[b] := preorder.length
    return (start, end, preorder)

dominated_blocks(index, block):
    require index.cfg_revision == current_cfg_revision
    require index.has(block)
    return index.preorder[index.start[block]:index.end[block]]
\end{verbatim}

The event stack makes pre- and post-actions explicit; it
uses linear space in the worst case, for example when the
entry immediately dominates thousands of blocks. A stack
holding one child cursor per ancestor can bound traversal
scratch by tree depth, although the published index still
contains one row per reachable block. If consumers expect
deterministic enumeration, build the child lists in a
specified order, such as the stable published block order.
That affects layout, not membership.

Keep the dominance frontier separate from the subtree
index. A join belongs to the frontier of \(L\) if \(L\)
dominates a predecessor of the join but does not strictly
dominate the join itself. In the original diamond, \(J\)
is in the frontiers of both \(L\) and \(R\); it is not in
either subtree. Frontiers are useful for finding potential
SSA merge points, and computing them needs CFG edges in
addition to the tree. The interval test can answer each
dominance predicate but does not enumerate the frontier by
slicing the preorder. On small graphs an additional oracle
for dominance is to temporarily omit \(a\) from entry
reachability and ask whether \(b\) becomes unreachable,
handling the entry and same-node cases directly.
